WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:05.400
Okay, so now to the next step.

00:05.560 --> 00:09.360
We have to go and monitor the duplicates in indexing.

00:09.360 --> 00:15.080
If you are working in a team with multiple developers and you are working parallelly in order to optimize

00:15.080 --> 00:21.800
the performance of the queries, what might happen is that different developers creating different indexes

00:21.960 --> 00:24.280
for the same column in the same table.

00:24.320 --> 00:29.320
But of course this must not happen if you have a clean and solid review process in the projects.

00:29.320 --> 00:31.840
But we are human and those things could happen.

00:31.840 --> 00:35.640
So that's why you have to monitor whether there are like duplicates.

00:35.800 --> 00:41.200
So the mission is to find whether there is a column that is involved in multiple indexes.

00:41.200 --> 00:43.680
So let's see how we can monitor that in SQL okay.

00:43.720 --> 00:48.680
So now it's very simple in order to find the duplicates of indexes inside your database.

00:48.680 --> 00:54.000
So we have learned before that we can find the list of all indexes in this table indexes in the system

00:54.000 --> 00:54.520
schema.

00:54.520 --> 00:57.720
And then we join it with the tables in order to get the table name.

00:57.720 --> 01:03.820
And then we have another table in order to find the columns that are involved in the index.

01:03.860 --> 01:07.100
Those informations we can find it inside the index columns.

01:07.140 --> 01:12.060
And now in order to get the full name of the columns we're going to go and join it with the columns

01:12.060 --> 01:12.580
table.

01:12.580 --> 01:14.300
So it's very simple and makes sense.

01:14.300 --> 01:16.500
Let's go and execute the whole query.

01:16.740 --> 01:20.140
Now as you can see it is sorted by the table name and the column name.

01:20.180 --> 01:23.300
And that's because we can find them easier the duplicate.

01:23.300 --> 01:25.260
So let's go and check the first table.

01:25.460 --> 01:30.340
So the country is part of this index where we have the column store non-clustered.

01:30.340 --> 01:35.380
And again the country is involved in another index where we have the customers country.

01:35.380 --> 01:38.860
And this is a row store non-clustered index.

01:38.860 --> 01:40.340
So this is of course bad thing.

01:40.340 --> 01:44.420
We have to go and decide now do we want it as a column store or row store.

01:44.460 --> 01:50.060
And if we check as well this table, we can find that the first name in two different clusters, the

01:50.060 --> 01:50.820
same story.

01:50.820 --> 01:54.500
And that's because we were practicing and creating those indexes and that's it.

01:54.500 --> 01:59.580
But now if you have like a large schema and a lot of indexes, I would go and make like a flag in order

01:59.580 --> 02:01.700
to understand whether we have a duplicate or not.

02:01.740 --> 02:07.960
And that's by calculating the number of rows of unique table name and index name, and we can do that

02:07.960 --> 02:10.240
very easily using the window functions.

02:10.240 --> 02:11.520
So let's have a new row.

02:11.520 --> 02:13.600
And we're going to go and use the function count.

02:13.640 --> 02:19.000
Since we want to find the number of rows over then we're going to go and partition by.

02:20.160 --> 02:24.840
We need the table name and as will the column name.

02:25.480 --> 02:27.920
Our expectation of this column should be one.

02:27.920 --> 02:30.760
If we have more than one, then there is an issue.

02:30.800 --> 02:34.680
And that means the column is inside two different indexes.

02:34.720 --> 02:38.600
And now let's go and sort it by the column name and descending.

02:39.040 --> 02:41.200
So let's go and execute it.

02:41.200 --> 02:46.760
And now we have here a nice flag where we can see how many rows we have for a specific column in a table.

02:46.760 --> 02:50.080
So if it's one like those columns they are fine.

02:50.080 --> 02:53.280
Those columns are involved only once in one index.

02:53.320 --> 02:57.840
But for the first four rows we have here an issue because we count here two columns.

02:57.840 --> 03:00.800
That means we have two indexes for the same column.

03:00.800 --> 03:02.400
So as you can see the query is very simple.

03:02.400 --> 03:05.200
And with that we have a nice report about that.

03:05.200 --> 03:08.040
Duplicates of indexes inside our database.
